"Centro-affine spectral geometry of polytopes (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21670)\nciteturn5academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Title: Centro-affine spectral geometry of polytopes ... IvakiDate: Fri Sep 18 12:06:32 2026In this paper, we develop a polyhedral version of Milman's centro-affine spectral framework for full-dimensional origin-symmetric polytopes.\nTitle: Centro-affine spectral geometry of polytopes\nAuthors: Yingxiang Hu, Mohammad N. Ivaki\nDate: Fri Sep 18 12:06:32 2026\n\nIn this paper, we develop a polyhedral version of Milman's centro-affine spectral framework for full-dimensional origin-symmetric polytopes. A weighted graph Laplacian on the facets plays the role of the smooth centro-affine Laplacian. For $n\\geq 3$, we prove that its first nonconstant even eigenvalue satisfies \\[ λ_{1,e}(P)\\geq n-1+\\frac{1}{n-1} \\] whenever $P$ is a local minimizer of $λ_{1,e}$ among origin-symmetric polytopes with the same facet normals, but not necessarily the same normal fan.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLogarithmic Brunn--Minkowski Inequality under $n-2$ Reflection Symmetries | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2608.20730)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 3 days ago; For origin-symmetric convex bodies in $\\R^n$R n invariant under $n-2$n−2 orthogonal hyperplanes reflections, we establish the logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski inequality and classify all equality cases without regularity assumptions. ... @misc{lu2026logarithmicbrunnminkowskiinequalityn2, title={Logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski Inequality under $n-2$ Reflection Symmetries}, author={XiaoRui Lu}, year={2026}, eprint={2608.20730}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.MG}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20730}, }\nSubmitted 28 Sept 2026\n\n# Logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski Inequality under $n-2$n−2 Reflection Symmetries\n\nXiaoRui Lu\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor origin-symmetric convex bodies in $\\R^n$R n invariant under $n-2$n−2 orthogonal hyperplanes reflections, we establish the logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski inequality and classify all equality cases without regularity assumptions. We derive the logarithmic Minkowski inequality and rigidity for cone-volume measures, including an explicit classification for bodies of revolution and uniqueness for simplicial polytopes in the prescribed symmetry class.\n\nView PaperPDF\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{lu2026logarithmicbrunnminkowskiinequalityn2, title={Logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski Inequality under $n-2$ Reflection Symmetries}, author={XiaoRui Lu}, year={2026}, eprint={2608.20730}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.MG}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20730}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLogarithmic Brunn--Minkowski Inequality under $n-2$ Reflection Symmetries (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20730)\nciteturn5academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Date: Fri Aug 21 04:22:15 2026We prove the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality for origin-symmetric convex bodies with symmetries to n-2 orthogonal hyperplanes, and discuss the equality case and the uniqueness of the related logarithmic Minkowski problem.\nTitle: Logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski Inequality under $n-2$ Reflection Symmetries\nAuthors: XiaoRui Lu\nDate: Fri Aug 21 04:22:15 2026\n\nWe prove the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality for origin-symmetric convex bodies with symmetries to n-2 orthogonal hyperplanes, and discuss the equality case and the uniqueness of the related logarithmic Minkowski problem. We also generalize the inequality from the Lebesgue measure to certain log-concave measure.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCentro-affine Poincaré inequality: Unconditional convex bodies (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.20223)\nciteturn5academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Title: Centro-affine Poincaré inequality: Unconditional convex bodiesAuthors: Yingxiang Hu, Mohammad N.IvakiDate: Wed Jul 22 14:45:30 2026\nTitle: Centro-affine Poincaré inequality: Unconditional convex bodies\nAuthors: Yingxiang Hu, Mohammad N. Ivaki\nDate: Wed Jul 22 14:45:30 2026\n\nWe prove that the centro-affine Poincaré inequality holds with constant $n$ for every $C^{\\infty}_+$ unconditional convex body and every smooth function with natural orthogonality conditions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeighted centro-affine Poincaré inequalities (https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.04774)\nciteturn5academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Date: Wed Jun  3 11:55:04 2026 ... We also establish an $L_0$-Brunn--Minkowski inequality for the $q$-th dual quermassintegral for $q\\in(0,n)$, with equality only for dilates, and an $L_p$-Brunn--Minkowski inequality for $q=n+α$ whenever \\[ 0<α\\le \\frac{2p(1-p)}{2-p}, \\] which in particular covers the range $q\\in(n,n+6-4\\sqrt{2}]$ for suitable $p\\in(0,1)$. ... Our main contribution is the introduction of a flat logarithmic centro-affine geometry on the positive orthant $(0,\\infty)^n$, adapted to the multiplicative structure of the $L_0$-sum.\nTitle: Weighted centro-affine Poincaré inequalities\nAuthors: Yingxiang Hu, Mohammad N. Ivaki\nDate: Wed Jun  3 11:55:04 2026\n\nWe obtain weighted centro-affine Bochner formulas on spherical caps associated with smooth strictly convex hypersurfaces. As a consequence, we prove weighted Poincaré inequalities on caps and on intersections of caps for a class of weights depending on the position vector $X$ of the hypersurface. In the unconditional case, we obtain a centro-affine Poincaré inequality with weight $|X|^2$, which is used to prove a Brunn--Minkowski inequality for the $(n+2)$-nd dual quermassintegral. We also establish an $L_0$-Brunn--Minkowski inequality for the $q$-th dual quermassintegral for $q\\in(0,n)$, with equality only for dilates, and an $L_p$-Brunn--Minkowski inequality for $q=n+α$ whenever \\[ 0<α\\le \\frac{2p(1-p)}{2-p}, \\] which in particular covers the range $q\\in(n,n+6-4\\sqrt{2}]$ for suitable $p\\in(0,1)$. These Brunn--Minkowski inequalities imply weighted centro-affine Poincaré inequalities and uniqueness results for the $L_{p,q}$-Minkowski problem in the unconditional class. Our main contribution is the introduction of a flat logarithmic centro-affine geometry on the positive orthant $(0,\\infty)^n$, adapted to the multiplicative structure of the $L_0$-sum. In this geometry, a Bochner formula yields a sharp Poincaré inequality, as well as a new proof of the centro-affine Poincaré inequality with constant $n$ due to Kolesnikov--Milman, for unconditional bodies and unconditional functions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCentro-affine spectral geometry of polytopes · Pith Review (https://pith.science/paper/2609.21670)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; \"Centro-affine spectral geometry of polytopes.\" arXiv:2609.21670 (2026). https://pith.science/paper/JEZ5Q5KC ... The main result is Theorem 1.1: for n≥3, if an origin-symmetric polytope is a local minimizer of λ_{1,e} among polytopes with the same facet normals, then λ_{1,e} ≥ n-1 + 1/(n-1). ... It bridges combinatorial and smooth geometry in a way that should interest people working on the log-Brunn–Minkowski conjecture or polyhedral spectral theory. ... Klartag, Logarithmic bounds for isoperimetry and slices of convex sets, Ars Inveniendi Analytica (2023), Paper No. 4, 17 pp., doi:10.15781/jsjy-0b06 https://doi.org/10.15781/jsjy-0b06\n\nREVIEW\n\n# Centro-affine spectral geometry of polytopes\n\nPaperPDF from arXiv\n\ndata files\n\nReviewThe objections and verdict, as JSON Paper breakdownThe authors' claims, conditions and measurements, as JSON Signed receiptProof this review has not been altered\n\nYingxiang Hu, Mohammad N. Ivaki\n\nMetric Geometry · Submitted 18 Sep 2026 · 27 references\n\nPith's verdict: A genuinely new polyhedral centro-affine spectral bound, conditional but internally solid.\n\nT0 · AI review ▾\n\nHow this review was made: An AI referee (deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash) read the paper on September 21, 2026 and answered a fixed list of questions about it. T0 is the starting level every paper gets. The levels describe how deeply Pith has checked the paper, never the authors.\n\n  1. T0 AI review An AI read the paper and answered a fixed list of questions.\n  2. T1 Audit Automatic checks that the references and files the paper relies on exist and match.\n  3. T2 Compute The paper's code or calculations re-run with the same results.\n  4. T3 Formal statement The claims written out in a formal math language a computer can read.\n  5. T4 Proof Those claims proved, and the proof checked by computer.\n\nHow Pith checks papers →\n\n· 1 major objection · 3 minor · Ask about this paper · Read the full review →\n\nChallenge this review Comment (0) Read on arXiv ↗ Cite ▾\n\narXiv ID2609.21670 (version 1)\n\n[Button: Copy]\n\nPith IDJEZ5Q5KC, Pith's permanent record of this paper and its review\n\n[Button: Copy]\n\nCitation Mohammad N. Ivaki, Yingxiang Hu. \"Centro-affine spectral geometry of polytopes.\" arXiv:2609.21670 (2026). https://pith.science/paper/JEZ5Q5KC\n\n## Why Pith says this\n\nHighlight any text to comment on it.\n\nEditor's note read the letter\n\nThis paper does something that sounds like it shouldn't work: it pulls Milman's smooth centro-affine spectral theory down to symmetric polytopes, replaces the smooth Laplacian with a weighted graph Laplacian on facets, and still gets a sharp lower bound. The main result is Theorem 1.1: for n≥3, if an origin-symmetric polytope is a local minimizer of λ_{1,e} among polytopes with the same facet normals, then λ_{1,e} ≥ n-1 + 1/(n-1). That's a clean, nontrivial bound, and the proof is the real contribution—it combines McMullen's Hodge–Riemann–Minkowski inequality with a careful Rayleigh quotient variation argument on the chamber volume polynomial. The paper is well organized, the notation is heavy but consistent, and every nontrivial step is cited or proved. The authors are honest about the main limitation: the theorem is conditional on the existence of a local minimizer. That's not a flaw in the math—it's an explicit hypothesis—but it does mean the result is more of a necessary condition than a universal bound. The paper also includes a useful upper-semicontinuity result and a midpoint inequality for the support Hessian that handles the non-simple chamber case. There is no circularity, no parameter fitting, and the derivation is self-contained modulo the cited inequalities. A few minor things: the extension to n=3 relies on vanishing of the fourth derivative (fine), and Remark 1.2 about multiplicity is stated without proof. But these are small. Overall, this is a serious piece of work. It bridges combinatorial and smooth geometry in a way that should interest people working on the log-Brunn–Minkowski conjecture or polyhedral spectral theory. I would send it out; it deserves a careful referee.\n\nWhat would settle it\n\nCompute $\\lambda_{1,e}$ numerically for an explicit origin-symmetric polytope with $n=3$, such as a cube or a regular octahedron. If the value is found to be less than $2.5$ (the bound $n-1+1/(n-1)$ for $n=3$) and the polytope can be shown to be a local minimizer in the prescribed class, then the theorem's conclusion would be contradicted. Conversely, if the bound holds for such examples, the result is supported.\n\nReferee report\n\nSignificance\n\nIf the central result is fully established, it provides a sharp conditional spectral gap for a discrete centro-affine Laplacian and transfers part of Milman's smooth centro-affine framework to polytopes, connecting the problem to mixed volumes, support-Hessian geometry, and Hodge-Riemann relations. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCentro-affine sp